{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $[-2,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-47922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-x+2)=-1+2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is belongs to the interval $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-2,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-47922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65efd17364ee2aa2964e3066de874e34796ce50768a433e5922f28916de6e051"} {"question" "q-47922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-47922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-47922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}-1}?$    \n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[0,1)$\n- $[-2,-1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0$ (it makes zero the denominator).   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval  $[0,1)$   is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,-1]$    is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,-1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65efd17364ee2aa2964e3066de874e34796ce50768a433e5922f28916de6e051"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-73487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/