eorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-129221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65efd17364ee2aa2964e3066de874e34796ce50768a433e5922f28916de6e051"} {"question" "q-129221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-129221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-129221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-131180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $[-2,0]$\n- $[0,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-131180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ba9cfb9f7b71aaf53cb700c22a0beb70799bd97e2fbf40c4614c78238f0cf68"} {"question" "q-131180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013819/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65efd1736